     "up": {"title":"Spot the true problem statements (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>These drafts are harder to judge. Some hide a solution inside the wording, some leave out the consequence, and some are only symptoms.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that is a complete, solution-neutral problem statement (who/what is affected, how, and the consequence).","lines":["1. Patients at a Roseau clinic wait an average of 70 minutes because paper files are hard to find; if this continues, some will stop attending follow-up appointments.","2. The Castries bakery has no online ordering system, so it is losing lunch-time customers.","3. Vendors at the Montego Bay craft market run out of best-selling items on cruise days because sales are never totalled.","4. A Basseterre courier firm delivers about 30 parcels a week late because routes are planned on paper the night before; if this continues, it will lose its contract with two online stores.","5. The Guyana rice mill needs to replace its old weighing scales to stop losing money.","6. Members of a St. George's credit union queue for up to 45 minutes on payday because only one teller handles loan payments; if nothing changes, members may move to a competing bank.","7. The computers at the Belize City library are slow.","8. Students in a Barbados sixth form lose up to 20 minutes of each IT lab because the shared laptops start slowly; if this continues, coursework deadlines will be missed and grades may fall."],"correct":[0,3,5,7],"win":"Excellent. Lines 1, 4, 6 and 8 give who/what, how and the consequence without naming a fix. Line 2 hides a solution (\"no online ordering system\"), line 3 has no consequence, line 5 names a fix and line 7 is only a symptom.","miss":"Check each line for all three parts, then check that it never names or implies a fix, such as \"has no online system\" or \"needs to replace\"."}
