     "up": {"title":"Optimal does not mean cheapest (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>The Grenada Nutmeg Export Cooperative has six warehouse staff with basic IT skills, a budget of EC$10,000, and a requirement that the system must work offline, because the warehouse Internet often fails.</p><ul><li><b>P</b>: cloud order system, EC$6,000 a year, very effective, online only</li><li><b>Q</b>: desktop order package, EC$8,500 once, effective, works offline, needs two days of training</li><li><b>R</b>: free spreadsheet, works offline, no automatic checks on orders</li><li><b>S</b>: custom-built system, EC$25,000</li></ul><p>Which option is optimal, with the STRONGEST justification?</p>","options":[{"t":"R, because it is free and works offline.","fb":"R meets the constraints but does not solve the problem: with no automatic checks, the errors continue."},{"t":"Q, because it meets the offline requirement that P fails, prevents the errors R allows, and fits the budget unlike S; its training can be done before go-live.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It compares Q with every alternative against the requirements and constraints, and manages Q's one weakness."},{"t":"P, because it is the most effective and costs less than Q.","fb":"P fails the offline requirement. An option that breaks a requirement cannot be optimal, however effective it is elsewhere."},{"t":"S, because a custom system would fit the cooperative exactly.","fb":"S is two and a half times the budget, so it breaks a constraint."}]}
