     "up": {"title":"Information for a personal decision (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Keisha, a sixth-former in Kingstown, St. Vincent, has two options: a scholarship to a university in Trinidad that covers <b>tuition only</b>, or a place at a local college that costs EC$6,000 a year. Her family can give her EC$8,000 a year.</p><p>Which item of information is MOST necessary before she can decide?</p>","options":[{"t":"The total yearly cost of living in Trinidad (rent, food, travel), to compare with the EC$8,000 her family can give","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The scholarship covers tuition only, so without living costs she cannot tell whether Trinidad is affordable at all. The decision cannot be made without it."},{"t":"Where each institution appears in an international league table","fb":"A ranking may influence her, but it does not tell her whether she can afford to go. It is not the most necessary item."},{"t":"Reviews from former students describing campus life in Trinidad","fb":"Reviews could help her choose, but she could still decide without them. They are useful, not necessary."},{"t":"The entry requirements of the local college","fb":"She already has a place at the college, so its entry requirements no longer affect her decision."}]}
