     "up": {"title":"Which criterion fails? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A Guyana health team is planning a 2026 nutrition campaign for schoolchildren in the Rupununi, an inland region where most families live in Indigenous villages and grow much of their own food. The team finds a refereed 2025 study of the diets of 3,000 children in Georgetown and other coastal towns. It was funded by a health charity that sells no products.</p><p>Which criterion gives the STRONGEST reason to be cautious about applying the study's findings directly to the Rupununi?</p>","options":[{"t":"Cultural context","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The study is refereed, recent and unbiased, but it describes coastal and urban children. Foods, incomes and eating habits differ in the Rupununi's Indigenous villages, so the findings must be adapted or local data collected."},{"t":"Bias","fb":"The funder has no products to sell and the study was refereed, so there is no clear motive to slant the findings."},{"t":"Currency","fb":"A 2025 study is recent enough for a 2026 campaign. Age is not the main concern."},{"t":"Accuracy","fb":"Nothing suggests errors, and 3,000 children is a large sample. The figures may be correct for the children who were studied."}]}
