     "up": {"title":"Scholarly or general audience? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder cards: some general-audience items quote experts, and one has references but no expert review. Decide who each publication was written for and how deep its analysis goes.</p>","items":[{"t":"A conference paper presented to coral reef scientists, with its methods, results and 40 references","bin":0,"fb":"Prepared for specialists and fully referenced: scholarly."},{"t":"A well-written feature in a regional airline's in-flight magazine, by a journalist who interviewed two coral scientists","bin":1,"fb":"Quoting experts does not change the audience. It is written for travellers: general audience."},{"t":"A book from a university press whose manuscripts are sent to outside experts before acceptance","bin":0,"fb":"A reputable academic publisher with outside review: scholarly."},{"t":"A newspaper article reporting a marine scientist's new findings, with no references","bin":1,"fb":"It tells the public that something was found but gives little of the evidence: general audience."},{"t":"A video by a qualified biologist explaining coral bleaching to secondary school students","bin":1,"fb":"The author is an expert, but the audience is the public and the treatment is simplified: general audience."},{"t":"The abstract of a refereed article on reef temperatures, found in a university library database","bin":0,"fb":"An abstract summarises a scholarly article written for specialists: scholarly."},{"t":"A self-published e-book by a dive instructor, with a short list of references at the end","bin":1,"fb":"A few references do not make it scholarly. It had no outside expert review and is written for recreational divers: general audience."},{"t":"A hotel group's press release announcing that the reef in front of its resort is \"thriving\"","bin":1,"fb":"Written to impress guests and the public, with no data: general audience, and possibly biased."}],"win":"Well judged. Level of scholarship depends on the intended audience and the depth of analysis, not on whether an expert is quoted or a few references appear. A newspaper quoting a scientist is still written for the public."}
