     "up": {"title":"Is this author an authority? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A student in Bridgetown is deciding whether to use a report on hurricane-resistant roofing. Some of the details below are close calls.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that shows the author has authority or knows the literature.","lines":["1. The author is a structural engineer who lectures on wind loads at a regional university.","2. Her three earlier papers on roof failures after major hurricanes appeared in refereed engineering journals.","3. A government review of the national building code cites her report.","4. A famous cricketer who has rebuilt his own house twice wrote the foreword.","5. The report lists 35 references and a bibliography of earlier wind-load studies.","6. She notes that engineers disagree about the best fastenings for older timber roofs and sets out both positions.","7. Every page carries the logo of a roofing manufacturer that paid for the report.","8. Most of her references are to her own blog posts.","9. The report has been downloaded 50,000 times."],"correct":[0,1,2,4,5],"win":"Correct. Lines 1, 2 and 3 show expertise, relevant publications and standing in the field; lines 5 and 6 show familiarity with the literature, including an acknowledged controversy. A celebrity foreword, a sponsor's logo (a bias warning), citing her own blog and download counts show neither.","miss":"Look again. Authority needs expertise in THIS field, relevant publications, citations by others and a reputable affiliation. Familiarity shows in references to other people's work and fair treatment of disagreement. A sponsor's logo is a bias warning, not a sign of authority."}
