     "up": {"title":"Timely and objective enough? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder judgements: in each case the research purpose decides whether the source is fit. Choose the BEST decision.</p>","items":[{"text":"Topic: how St. Lucia's banana industry changed after its special access to the UK market ended. Source: a 1995 trade report on banana exports.","options":["Historically relevant: accept it as evidence of the earlier period","Outdated: reject it","Subjective: reject it"],"answer":0,"fb":"Research on change over time needs evidence from before the change. A 1995 report is valuable evidence of that period."},{"text":"Topic: which antivirus software a Grenada accounting firm should buy this year. Source: a refereed 2019 comparison of antivirus products.","options":["Reject: refereed articles are too technical","Likely outdated: threats and products change quickly, so find current tests","Accept: it is refereed"],"answer":1,"fb":"Refereeing does not keep information current. In IT security, a 2019 comparison is likely outdated for a purchase today."},{"text":"Topic: how Barbados residents feel about a proposed tax on sugary drinks. Source: letters to the editor of a Barbadian newspaper from 12 residents.","options":["Accept as views, but balance them with a wider sample, because 12 letter writers may not represent residents","Reject: they are subjective","Accept them as objective evidence of public opinion"],"answer":0,"fb":"Research on views needs subjective sources, but a handful of self-selected writers may not represent the public. Balance and widen the sample."},{"text":"Topic: whether a new desalination plant in Antigua meets drinking-water standards. Source: the operator's press release saying the water is \"crystal clear and perfectly safe\".","options":["Rely on objective laboratory results, and treat the press release as a biased lead","Accept: it is current","Accept: the operator knows the plant best"],"answer":0,"fb":"Water safety is an empirical question, and the operator gains from a good report. Laboratory results are the evidence; the press release is only a lead."},{"text":"Topic: comparing stakeholder views on Jamaica's data protection law. Sources: a consultant's opinion column and the published text of the law itself.","options":["Use only the column, because the research is about views","Use both: the column as one view, and the law's text as the objective record of what the law says","Use only the law's text, because opinions must be rejected"],"answer":1,"fb":"The research compares views, so the column is needed, but describing what the law says needs its objective text. Good research uses each source for its purpose."},{"text":"Topic: the history of computers in Trinidad and Tobago's secondary schools. Source: a 2025 anonymous blog post with no references.","options":["Accept: it is recent, so it is timely","Accept: historical research can use any source","Treat with caution: it is recent but un-refereed and unsupported, so check it against older primary sources"],"answer":2,"fb":"For history, being recent is no advantage, and an anonymous, unreferenced post gives nothing to check. Verify it against records from the period."}],"win":"Excellent. You judged each source against the purpose of the research: old can be right for history, refereed can still be outdated, views need balancing, and empirical questions need objective evidence."}
