     "up": {"title":"Subjective sources: accept or reject? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Darnell, a CAPE IT student in Port of Spain, is researching two questions for one project:</p><p>(1) What do taxi drivers, passengers and the transport ministry think about app-based taxi services?<br>(2) How much does a 10 km trip cost by app taxi compared with a regular taxi?</p><p>He has opinion columns by a taxi association leader and a passenger group, and the price lists published by two app companies. Which plan is BEST?</p>","options":[{"t":"Reject the columns because they are subjective, and answer both questions from the price lists.","fb":"Question 1 is about views, so the subjective sources are the very evidence he needs. Price lists cannot tell him what people think."},{"t":"Use only the passenger group's column for question 1, because passengers are the people most affected.","fb":"Choosing one stakeholder's view brings bias into research that is meant to compare views."},{"t":"Use the columns for question 1, labelled as views and balanced with a statement from the ministry; use the price lists for question 2, checked against a few real trip receipts.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Research on views needs subjective sources, balanced across stakeholders. The cost question is empirical, so it needs objective prices, checked because companies may advertise their lowest rates."},{"t":"Use the columns for both questions, because the taxi association leader knows the trade well.","fb":"Expertise does not turn opinion into measurement. Question 2 is empirical and needs objective prices."}]}
