     "up": {"title":"Spot the warning signs (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A CAPE student in Kingston is checking a web page that claims a new wristband prevents dengue. Some details sound alarming but are not warning signs, and some warning signs are well hidden.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that is a good reason to reject or doubt this information.","lines":["1. The page is on the website that sells the wristband, but the article is styled as \"independent health news\".","2. It quotes \"a study by leading scientists\" but gives no names, journal or link.","3. The same paragraph appears word for word on 15 other websites.","4. It was the first result in the student's search.","5. The footer shows \"© 2018\" and nothing later, yet the article talks about \"this year's outbreak\".","6. It has a photo of a smiling family in a garden.","7. It links to the Ministry of Health's page of dengue advice.","8. All the customer reviews give five stars and show first names only.","9. It is written in clear, simple English."],"correct":[0,1,2,4,7],"win":"Correct. A sales site dressed as news (bias), an unnamed \"study\" (accuracy), copied text that only looks like confirmation, a 2018 date on \"this year's\" news (currency) and unverifiable reviews are reasons to doubt. Search position, a photo, clear English and a link to the ministry are not.","miss":"Look again. Ask who published it and why, whether the evidence can be checked, whether the copies are really independent, and whether the date fits the content. Search ranking and clear writing prove nothing either way."}
