     "up": {"title":"Good signs on a web page (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A student in Belmopan finds a page on keeping food safe during power cuts, written for small restaurants. Some details look reassuring but prove little.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY line that is a GOOD sign that the page can be accepted.","lines":["1. The page is on www.belizefoodsafety.org, and its \"About us\" page names the board and gives a street address.","2. The article names its author, a food safety officer, and shows \"Last updated: 3 August 2026\".","3. The footer shows \"© 2026\", but the article describes rules \"introduced last year, in 2019\".","4. A note says every page is reviewed by the organisation's technical committee before it is posted.","5. It links to the regional public health agency's food safety guidelines.","6. The Ministry of Health's page for restaurant owners links to this article.","7. It appeared as the first sponsored result in a search.","8. Hundreds of comments below the article thank the author.","9. A \"Contact the Webmaster\" form appears at the bottom of the page."],"correct":[0,1,3,4,5,8],"win":"Correct. An identifiable publisher, a named author with an update date, an approval policy, reliable links in both directions and a Webmaster contact are good signs. A current © year does not make 2019 content current, and sponsored ranking and friendly comments prove nothing.","miss":"Look again. Ask who published it, whether it was approved, when the CONTENT was last updated (not just the footer), and which reliable pages link to and from it."}
