     "up": {"title":"Links to and from a page (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Two pages give advice on storing drinking water before a hurricane.</p><p><b>Page P</b> is linked from the Antigua disaster office's preparedness page and from a university library guide, but shows no author or date.<br><b>Page Q</b> names its author, shows \"Last updated: June 2026\" and links to the regional public health agency, but no reliable site links to it.</p><p>Which conclusion is BEST?</p>","options":[{"t":"Page P is more acceptable because reliable sites link to it, so no further checks are needed.","fb":"Links from authorities are strong support for P, but its missing author and date still need checking. \"No further checks\" goes too far."},{"t":"Both have strengths: trusted sites linking to P suggest authorities rely on it, while Q is stronger on authorship and currency. Use P after checking that it is current, and confirm Q against the health agency's advice.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. You weighed the evidence: links from authorities are strong support, but authorship and currency still matter, so each page needs a different check before use."},{"t":"Page Q is more acceptable, because links from a page always matter more than links to it.","fb":"The reverse is often true: links to a page from reliable sites may matter more, because anyone can add a link to a trusted site."},{"t":"Neither can be used, because web pages are un-refereed.","fb":"Un-refereed is not the same as unusable. The criteria exist to decide when online information can be accepted."}]}
