     "up": {"title":"The sargassum report (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are now the lead researcher at an environmental consultancy in Bridgetown. Twelve beach-front hotels want a report advising whether to invest in floating sargassum barriers. Some choices only half-work.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The hotels want a recommendation within three weeks. How do you begin?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a wide web search and keep everything that mentions sargassum barriers.","to":"bad1","fb":"Without a list of what is needed, extraneous material buries what matters."},{"t":"List the essential information (arrival forecasts, barrier costs and performance, health guidance, the hotels' own clean-up costs), note what is desirable, then plan where to find each item.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Deciding what is essential first focuses the search and the budget."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A barrier manufacturer sends a glossy brochure claiming its barrier \"stops 95% of seaweed on any beach\", with a case study from Florida. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Quote the 95% figure as the barrier's performance, since the brochure is current.","to":"bad2","fb":"Current is not the same as unbiased or relevant. The seller gains from the claim, and Florida is not Barbados."},{"t":"Reject the whole brochure because the manufacturer is biased.","to":"ok1","fb":"Bias is real here, but rejecting everything also throws away accurate specifications and prices you need."},{"t":"Use its technical specifications and prices, but treat the 95% claim as biased and from a different setting, and look for independent tests on Caribbean beaches.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong judgement. You separated checkable facts from persuasion and flagged the cultural context."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>You find (A) a refereed 2024 study measuring barrier performance on four Caribbean islands, (B) a refereed 2015 study from Mexico's Caribbean coast and (C) a hotel manager's video of a barrier \"working perfectly\" last week. How do you weigh them?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Rely mainly on A (refereed, current, regional); use B to show how designs have changed; treat C as un-refereed eyewitness evidence to confirm with that hotel's own figures.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. You weighed refereeing, currency and context together and gave each source a role."},{"t":"Use A and B equally, because both are refereed.","to":"ok2","fb":"Both are refereed, but B is eleven years older and barrier designs have changed. Currency matters as well."},{"t":"Rely mainly on C, because it is the most recent.","to":"bad3","fb":"One manager's video is un-refereed and covers one week at one beach. Recent is not the same as reliable."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Your draft is ready. The hotels ask you to add two items: one owner's statement that \"our guests don't mind the seaweed\", with no survey behind it, and the hotels' monthly clean-up costs for the past two years. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add the clean-up costs as essential evidence; treat the owner's statement as cosmetic and either survey guests or leave it out.","to":"n5","fb":"Right. Real figures go in; a claim that hides missing evidence does not."},{"t":"Add both, since both come from the hotels themselves.","to":"bad4","fb":"Coming from the client does not make a claim evidence. The statement masks the missing guest survey."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The figures suggest barriers would pay for themselves in about three years at most hotels, but study A warns that barriers can fail in heavy swells. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Recommend barriers for every hotel and leave out the swell warning so the report reads more clearly.","to":"bad5","fb":"Leaving out the warning makes the report incomplete and one-sided, exactly the faults you rejected in the brochure."},{"t":"Recommend barriers for hotels on sheltered bays, state the payback figures and the swell risk, and set a review after one season comparing clean-up costs with the two-year baseline.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. A complete, balanced recommendation with a measurable review."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Trusted report.</b> Hotels on sheltered bays install barriers and cut clean-up costs; exposed hotels avoid a costly failure. You chose essential information first, separated fact from persuasion, weighed refereeing against currency and context, refused a cosmetic claim and reported the risks completely.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Partly useful.</b> The report has no reliable cost figures, so the hotels cannot judge payback. Lesson: a biased source may still contain accurate specifications; separate facts from persuasion rather than rejecting everything.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Partly useful.</b> The report averages old and new results and understates modern barriers' performance. Lesson: refereed status does not make an older study current; weigh currency as well.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Information overload.</b> After a week you have hundreds of pages and still no barrier costs. Try again: decide what is essential before you search.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Misleading figure.</b> Hotels expect 95% protection; on local beaches the barriers catch far less and the hotels lose confidence in your firm. Try again: check a seller's claim for bias and context.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Weak evidence.</b> Your recommendation rests on one video. When a swell breaks a barrier at another hotel, you have nothing to support your advice. Try again: rely on refereed, measured evidence.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Cosmetic claim exposed.</b> A guest survey by a newspaper shows strong complaints about the smell, and your report's claim is quoted against you. Try again: cosmetic information hides what is missing.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Incomplete advice.</b> Barriers at two exposed hotels break in the first heavy swell, and the hotels ask why the risk was not reported. Try again: complete information includes the risks.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
