     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> First, the <b>cruise ship arrival schedule</b> is essential, because Mr. Croes can only benefit from moving to Oranjestad on days when ships are in port; without it he cannot know which days to move. Second, his <b>daily sales for the past year</b> are essential, because he must compare current earnings at Palm Beach and Eagle Beach with what he might earn in town; without them he cannot judge whether moving would improve sales. Third, the <b>vending permit fee</b> in Oranjestad is essential, because it is a cost that must be subtracted from any extra income; if the fee is higher than the extra profit, moving would be the wrong decision. (The number of competing stalls near the pier is also essential, as heavy competition could reduce his share of customers.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Desirable:</b> customer comments on his social media page, because they could show why sales fell and what customers want, improving the decision, but he could still decide without them. <b>Extraneous:</b> his daughter's song lyrics (or the history of US food trucks), because they have no bearing on where to park. <b>Cosmetic:</b> the friend's assurance that \"everybody in Oranjestad will buy from you\", because it looks like useful demand information but masks the missing real information (how many customers there are and how much they spend), so it cannot guide the decision; more information is needed to avoid a wrong decision.</p><p><b>(c)</b> This is a <b>commercial</b> problem, because it concerns a business trying to improve its sales and profit.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>Award each essential mark only when the item is <b>linked to the decision</b>; \"sales figures\" alone is an identification, not an explanation. Accept the competitor count as an alternative essential item. In (b), the reason must match the category definition: candidates often call the friend's assurance \"desirable\"; it should not earn the mark unless they show that it only looks useful while masking the missing customer figures. Point out that the stimulus deliberately includes distractors, just as Paper 02 scenarios do.</p>",
