      "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> A refereed source is checked by <b>independent experts</b> before it is <b>published</b>. The refereed source is <b>Source 1</b>, the marine science journal article.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Source 2 has a problem with <b>bias</b> because the manufacturer makes money from selling plastic bottles, so it will only say good things about them. It is also undated.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Source 3 has a problem with <b>accuracy</b> because no photographer is named and the photo could be old, from another beach or edited; shares do not prove it is true. The students could check it by visiting Fort James Beach themselves and taking dated photos.</p><p><b>(d)</b> 2010 is a problem of <b>currency</b> because the report is out of date and prices and attitudes to plastic have changed. The United Kingdom is a problem of <b>cultural context</b> because English supermarkets have different laws, prices and shopping habits from Antigua.</p><p><b>(e)</b> On the Internet, anyone can <b>publish without an editor or expert checking it</b>, so the students should <b>check the author and date and confirm the facts with an independent source</b>, such as Source 1.</p>",
      "teacherTip": "<p>The dial-down gives the criterion names and splits each source into \"name it, then give a reason\". When a student succeeds, move them to the regular question, where they must choose the criterion themselves and explain the effect on the proposal.</p>"
