     "up": {"title":"Justify the choice (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A St. Kitts tourism board wants touch-screen kiosks for cruise visitors. Board members cannot agree on what the screens should show and have never used such a system. Once the design is agreed, they want the real system built under a fixed-price contract.</p><p>Which justification would earn the MOST marks?</p>","options":[{"t":"An evolutionary prototype should be refined until it becomes the final system, because the board wants a fixed price.","fb":"A fixed price favours specifying the real build after the prototype. Growing an unplanned prototype into the product makes cost hard to fix."},{"t":"Prototyping, because it is flexible and users like it.","fb":"Generic: no evidence from the scenario and no consequence, so it earns little."},{"t":"Agile, because board members will work with the team every day.","fb":"Nothing in the scenario says board members are available daily. A justification must use the evidence given."},{"t":"A throwaway prototype should come first: because members cannot agree on or describe the screens, they and some visitors can try a quick model and settle the layout; the fixed-price build can then be specified accurately and the prototype discarded.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It uses two pieces of evidence (no agreement on screens, a fixed-price build), links each to a feature (trying a model, discarding it once requirements are clear) and states the consequence."}]}
