     "up": {"title":"Command words in action (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: all six answers are about Leeward Ferries' booking system and look alike. Match each command word to the answer that does exactly what it asks, no more and no less.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Distinguish","r":"\"In prototyping, requirements are discovered by users trying a model, whereas in Waterfall they are fixed and signed off before design.\""},{"l":"Describe","r":"\"Agile builds software in short time-boxed iterations, each delivering working features, with a customer representative working alongside the team.\""},{"l":"State an advantage","r":"\"Cost can be estimated at the start.\""},{"l":"Explain a disadvantage","r":"\"In Agile the scope keeps changing, so the ferry company cannot be given a final price, which makes it hard to budget for the project.\""},{"l":"Justify","r":"\"The timetable changes every season (evidence); Agile reprioritises the backlog each sprint (feature); so new routes can be added within weeks (consequence).\""},{"l":"Evaluate","r":"\"Agile suits the changing timetable, but its cost is unpredictable; capping the project at ten sprints would keep it within budget.\""}],"win":"Well matched. \"State\" wants the bare point; \"explain\" adds the consequence; \"describe\" gives key characteristics; \"distinguish\" contrasts one feature with \"whereas\"; \"justify\" links evidence, feature and consequence; \"evaluate\" weighs a strength against a managed drawback."}
