     "up": {"title":"Engineered or improvised? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder: every note below sounds professional at first. Decide whether each one is genuinely systematic and disciplined, or still informal underneath.</p>","items":[{"t":"Requirements are agreed with the bursar, but a programmer then adds three extra screens she thinks the school will like, without asking","bin":1,"fb":"Agreed requirements mean little if scope is changed without approval. This is informal."},{"t":"The test plan lists each fee amount with its expected and actual result, including the boundary values of US$0 and the maximum fee","bin":0,"fb":"Planned tests with expected results and boundary data: software engineering."},{"t":"Before coding, the database tables are designed and the bursar checks that every field on the paper receipt is included","bin":0,"fb":"A design checked against the requirements before building is disciplined practice."},{"t":"The programmer tests the system only with the payments she expects parents to make","bin":1,"fb":"Testing only expected (normal) data misses boundary and erroneous cases. Still trial and error."},{"t":"A second developer reads each module against the coding standards before it is accepted","bin":0,"fb":"Code review against standards is a quality-control practice."},{"t":"A fix is made directly on the live system during school hours because \"it is only one line\"","bin":1,"fb":"No testing and no change control: the opposite of engineering, however small the fix."},{"t":"Every change request from the school is logged, estimated and approved before work starts","bin":0,"fb":"Controlled change is a core engineering practice."},{"t":"The technical manual is promised for \"after the project\", and the contract ends without one","bin":1,"fb":"A promised manual that never appears leaves the school dependent on one developer."},{"t":"The team times each payment on the school's oldest office computer and compares the result with the requirement of under five seconds","bin":0,"fb":"Measuring performance against a stated requirement is engineering, not guesswork."},{"t":"Code comments are written in shorthand that only the original programmer understands","bin":1,"fb":"Comments nobody else can read do not make code maintainable."}],"win":"Sharp judgement. Engineering is not just having requirements, tests and documents; it is controlling them: agreed scope, planned tests with boundary and erroneous data, reviewed code, approved changes, measured performance and documentation that someone else can use."}
