     "up": {"title":"Engineering or just programming? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A Paper 02 question asks: \"<b>Explain</b> why a hospital in Port of Spain should use a software engineering approach for its new patient-records system. (3 marks)\"</p><p>Which answer would earn ALL three marks?</p>","options":[{"t":"\"The hospital should use software engineering because it is the modern way to write code in a high-level language.\"","fb":"This confuses software engineering with programming and gives no reason linked to the hospital."},{"t":"\"Software engineering means buying the best hardware so that the software never fails.\"","fb":"Buying hardware is not software engineering, and no approach guarantees software that never fails."},{"t":"\"Software engineering applies a systematic, disciplined approach to developing and maintaining software. A patient-records system is large, used by many staff and holds sensitive data, so errors could harm patients. Documented requirements, planned testing and technical manuals reduce errors and let other developers maintain it for years.\"","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It defines the approach (1), explains why the hospital needs it: size, sensitive data and the cost of failure (1), and shows how it is applied: requirements, testing, manuals (1)."},{"t":"\"Software engineering is the systematic, disciplined approach to developing software.\"","fb":"A definition only: one mark at most. It does not say why the hospital needs it or how it would be applied."}]}
