     "up": {"title":"Match the design item to its purpose (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: the Harbour Ferry Line design specification now has ten items, and several look alike. Match each design item to the part of the system it specifies.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A rule that a passenger's phone number must have 7 to 10 digits","r":"Input validation"},{"l":"A diagram showing that one sailing has many bookings and each booking belongs to one passenger","r":"Data storage: entities and relationships"},{"l":"A decision table listing every combination of child, senior and resident, with the fare for each","r":"Processing rules with several conditions"},{"l":"\"SailingID: text, 5 characters, unique, for example PH001\"","r":"Description of a data item"},{"l":"A menu map showing how a clerk moves from the home screen to refunds","r":"Navigation in the user interface"},{"l":"A nightly copy of the bookings database to a server on another island","r":"Security: backup procedures"},{"l":"Ticket clerks can sell tickets, but only the manager can issue refunds","r":"Security: access levels"},{"l":"The system will run as a web app on a cloud server, written in Java","r":"Platform and programming language"},{"l":"A layout of the end-of-day sales report, with totals by route","r":"Output design"},{"l":"Pseudocode for working out the number of free seats on a sailing","r":"Processing logic for one task"}],"win":"Correct. Design specifies inputs and validation, data storage, processing (single tasks and multi-condition rules), the interface, security (access and backup), outputs and the platform, using tools such as the data dictionary, decision tables and pseudocode."}
