     "up": {"title":"Logical, physical or prototype? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder items from Harbour Ferry Line's design work. Ask: does it name real technology (physical design), describe only what is needed (logical design), or show users inspecting a model (prototype)?</p>","items":[{"t":"Each passenger may have many bookings, but each booking belongs to exactly one passenger","bin":0,"fb":"A rule about the data, with no technology chosen: logical design."},{"t":"PassengerID is stored as an integer that the database generates automatically","bin":1,"fb":"A data type and a database feature: physical design."},{"t":"The system must be able to find all the bookings for a given sailing","bin":0,"fb":"A process that is needed, described without technology: logical design."},{"t":"The query that lists bookings uses an index on SailingID so that it runs faster","bin":1,"fb":"An index is a technical storage decision: physical design."},{"t":"A process takes a fare type and a journey and returns the price","bin":0,"fb":"Inputs and output of a process, with no platform: logical design."},{"t":"Prices are stored in the Fares table as decimal numbers with two decimal places","bin":1,"fb":"A table, a field and a data type: physical design."},{"t":"Ferry clerks use a clickable mock-up for a morning and say the refund button is hard to find","bin":2,"fb":"Users inspecting a working model before development: prototype."},{"t":"The booking screen will be built as a web page that adjusts to tablets used at the dock","bin":1,"fb":"An actual interface and platform decision: physical design."},{"t":"Three cruise passengers try a paper sketch of the ticket-buying steps and get confused at the payment step","bin":2,"fb":"Even a simple model that customers inspect before development counts as a prototype."},{"t":"Each passenger's nationality must be recorded for the immigration report","bin":0,"fb":"What data is needed, and why, without saying how it is stored: logical design."}],"win":"Well sorted. Logical design states what is needed; physical design is the blueprint with real technology (types, tables, indexes, platform); prototypes, however simple, let customers inspect before development."}
