     "up": {"title":"Match the changeover to the situation (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: five situations, including both kinds of phasing. Match each one to the changeover method being used.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A Trinidad supermarket chain closes on Christmas night, removes the old tills and opens on Boxing Day with only the new system.","r":"Direct changeover"},{"l":"A Barbados ministry runs its old and new payroll for two months, checking every civil servant's payslip on both.","r":"Parallel running"},{"l":"A Jamaican hotel group moves reservations to the new system in May, housekeeping in July and billing in September.","r":"Phased by application"},{"l":"A credit union moves its Castries branch first; its Vieux Fort and Gros Islet branches follow once Castries is stable.","r":"Phased by location"},{"l":"A pharmacy keeps its old system running while the new system's stock and prescription functions run alongside it until fully tested, then switches completely.","r":"Pilot running"}],"win":"Correct. Direct replaces everything at once; parallel runs the whole of both systems until fault free; phased replaces parts gradually, either by application (module by module) or by location (site by site); pilot runs key parts alongside the old system until fully tested."}
