     "up": {"title":"Steer the project (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are the systems analyst for Island Wheels Car Rentals, which has branches at the airport and in Bridgetown. This time more of the decisions are close calls, and some choices only half-work.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The owner says: \"My nephew can code. Let him start building tomorrow.\" What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Define the problem and run a feasibility study comparing a rental package, a custom system and improving the paper cards, ending in a go/no-go recommendation.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Alternatives are compared against all five feasibility types before money is committed."},{"t":"Let the nephew start, but ask him to write down the requirements as he goes.","to":"ok1","fb":"Writing requirements during coding is better than none, but nobody has checked whether a custom build is even the best option."},{"t":"Agree: starting to code early saves time.","to":"bad1","fb":"Coding without a feasibility study or requirements risks building the wrong system."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The study recommends a rental package. In analysis, the branch managers describe what it must do. Which requirements specification is BEST?</p>","choices":[{"t":"\"The system must be fast, modern and easy to use.\"","to":"bad2","fb":"None of these can be tested, so nobody can prove the system meets them."},{"t":"Functional: book a car, record returns, share one fleet between both branches. Non-functional: a booking confirmed within five seconds, available 24 hours a day, only managers can change prices.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Specific, testable functional and non-functional requirements."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Every planned test passes. In acceptance testing, airport staff find that the system cannot record the fuel level when a car is returned, which they charge customers for. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Go live now and add fuel recording after the season.","to":"ok2","fb":"The deadline is met, but staff must work around a missing function during the busiest months."},{"t":"Treat it as a validation failure: add the requirement, configure and test it, run regression tests, and repeat acceptance testing before go-live.","to":"n4","fb":"Right. A missed requirement must flow back through design, development and testing."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Bookings are critical and the tourist season starts in three weeks. Which changeover method do you put in the conversion plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Parallel running at both branches for the whole season.","to":"ok3","fb":"Very safe, but doing every booking twice at both branches during peak season overloads staff."},{"t":"Phase it by location: Bridgetown first, keeping backups of the paper cards, then the airport once Bridgetown is stable.","to":"n5","fb":"Sensible. Problems are found at the smaller branch while the busy airport continues safely."},{"t":"Direct changeover at both branches on the first day of the season.","to":"bad3","fb":"The riskiest method at the worst possible time."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>The system has been live at both branches for four months. The owner asks whether the project was a success. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tell him it is working, so nothing more is needed.","to":"bad4","fb":"Without review, hidden problems and missed objectives go unnoticed."},{"t":"Review it: compare double bookings and lost reservations with the proposal's objectives, survey staff and customers, compare actual costs with the estimates, and write an evaluation report.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Review measures the objectives with evidence and feeds the next cycle."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The review shows double bookings have stopped and costs matched the estimates, but airport staff want a faster way to record returned cars. The evaluation report recommends this enhancement, starting a new, smaller cycle. You produced every deliverable and made each decision from evidence.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Partly successful.</b> The nephew's system works for one branch, but it cost more than a package that already handled two branches. Lesson: the feasibility study exists to compare alternatives before anything is built.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Partly successful.</b> The system goes live on time, but staff track fuel on paper all season and some charges are lost. Lesson: a validation failure found before go-live is cheapest to fix then.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Partly successful.</b> No data is lost, but staff work late every night entering bookings twice, and two resign. Lesson: weigh the workload of parallel running against the risk, and consider phasing by location.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong system.</b> Three months later the nephew's program cannot let two branches share the same cars, and nobody wrote down what it was meant to do. Try again and begin with feasibility and analysis.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Untestable.</b> At handover, the owner says the system is not \"fast\" enough, and nobody can prove otherwise. Try again: write specific, measurable requirements.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Chaos at the airport.</b> A database error on day one loses forty reservations, and there is no fallback. Try again and choose a lower-risk changeover.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Hidden problems.</b> Staff quietly return to paper cards for some tasks, and the owner never finds out why. Try again and evaluate the system properly.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
