     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> A feasibility study is a short early investigation that decides whether a proposed system is worth developing and can be developed, before large sums are spent. It compares alternative solutions and produces a <b>system proposal</b> recommending whether and how to proceed. <b>Economic feasibility</b> asks whether the benefits outweigh the costs, using a cost-benefit analysis of development and running costs against tangible and intangible benefits; for example, the Carenage Water Taxi compares a USD 18,000 system with USD 9,000 a year of cash-handling savings. <b>Operational feasibility</b> asks whether the system fits existing procedures; for example, whether scanning e-tickets fits the current boarding routine at the pier and the way managers close the day. (Whether long-serving captains can adapt to scanning e-tickets is social/behavioural feasibility.)</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Design</b> produces <b>diagrams and charts</b> such as an ER diagram and screen layouts. Their purpose is to specify precisely how the system will store data and interact with users, so that programmers can build it and users can check it. <b>Development</b> produces the <b>tested system with its completed test plan</b>, whose purpose is to provide evidence that every requirement has been tested with normal, boundary and erroneous data and works correctly. <b>Implementation</b> produces the <b>conversion plan</b>, whose purpose is to control the changeover: it sets the method (for example, parallel running), the timetable, data conversion, training and fallback arrangements, so that the business is not disrupted.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A user manual is written for end users such as cashiers, whereas a technical manual is written for IT staff and programmers. A user manual uses plain language and contains step-by-step instructions, screenshots and explanations of error messages, whereas a technical manual contains technical detail such as installation steps, the data dictionary, diagrams and commented program code. The user manual's purpose is to help people operate the system correctly, whereas the technical manual's purpose is to allow the system to be maintained and upgraded.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "Students often confuse economic and operational feasibility, and often name \"programming\" as a deliverable of implementation. Under the class definitions, staff resistance is social/behavioural feasibility; if a student puts it under operational, credit it only where the explanation is about fit with procedures. Insist on the syllabus deliverable names and on two-sided \"whereas\" points for (c).",
