      "model": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b> A feasibility study decides whether the proposed system is worth developing and can be developed. <b>(ii)</b> It produces the system proposal.</p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Economic feasibility checks whether the benefits of the system outweigh its costs. <b>(ii)</b> Operational feasibility checks whether the system fits the existing procedures, the way the business already works. <b>(iii)</b> In the scenario, the system costs USD 18,000 but saves USD 9,000 a year, which is economic feasibility; tickets still being checked at the boarding gate as now is operational feasibility.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Design produces the design specification, made of diagrams and charts such as an ER diagram. Development produces the coded system, test plans and the technical manual. Implementation produces the conversion plan.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A user manual is written for end users such as ticket sellers, whereas a technical manual is written for IT staff and programmers who maintain the system.</p>",
