     "up": {"title":"Table or tree? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Island Link Ferries' new fare policy has three yes/no conditions: <b>Under 12?</b>, <b>Return ticket?</b> and <b>Resident of St. Vincent and the Grenadines?</b> Under-12s pay the child fare and everyone else the adult fare; return tickets get 10% off; residents get a further 15% off.</p>","items":[{"text":"How many rules does the full decision table for this policy need?","options":["3","9","6","8"],"answer":3,"fb":"2 to the power 3 = 8 rules."},{"text":"A 35-year-old Saba resident buys a return ticket. Which actions apply?","options":["Adult fare only","Adult fare + 10% off + 15% resident discount","Child fare + 15% resident discount","Adult fare + 10% off"],"answer":1,"fb":"Under 12: N; Return: Y; Resident: Y. All three answers add up to adult fare with both discounts."},{"text":"A 7-year-old tourist buys a single ticket. Which actions apply?","options":["Child fare + 10% off","Adult fare","Child fare + 15% off","Child fare only"],"answer":3,"fb":"Under 12: Y; Return: N; Resident: N. Only the child fare applies."},{"text":"Drawn as a full tree that asks all three questions on every path, how many leaves does it have?","options":["6","8","16","3"],"answer":1,"fb":"Each leaf matches one rule of the table: 8 leaves."},{"text":"The ferry company's board, who are not technical, must approve the policy. Which tool should the analyst present, and why?","options":["Pseudocode, because it is closest to the program","A Gantt chart, because it shows timing","A decision table, because boards prefer grids","A decision tree, because they can follow each branch visually"],"answer":3,"fb":"A tree is visual and easy for non-technical readers to follow."},{"text":"Before coding, the programmer wants to be sure no combination has been forgotten. Which tool is BEST, and why?","options":["A decision tree, because it shows the order of questions","A decision table, because counting the 8 columns proves every combination is covered","A data flow diagram, because it shows data movement","A questionnaire, because it gathers numbers"],"answer":1,"fb":"Completeness is easy to check in a table: count the 2 to the power n columns."},{"text":"If the policy grew to seven yes/no conditions, how many rules would the full table need?","options":["64","128","14","49"],"answer":1,"fb":"2 to the power 7 = 128. A tree with 128 leaves would be very cluttered; a table stays more manageable."}],"win":"Excellent. You traced a three-condition policy, counted rules and leaves with 2 to the power n, and chose a tree for approval and a table for proving completeness."}
