     "up": {"title":"Which design tool is this? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder items from Island Code Studio's design work for Harbourside Hotel. Several pairs look alike, so check the details: variables and arrows, capitalised keywords for users, grids, branches or field definitions.</p>","items":[{"t":"RoomType: text, 6 characters; allowed values Single, Double or Suite; used on the booking form and in the Rooms table","bin":0,"fb":"Type, size and allowed values for one data item, shared across the system: data dictionary."},{"t":"IF guest stays more than 7 nights THEN apply the weekly rate OTHERWISE apply the nightly rate, read and signed off by the front-office manager","bin":1,"fb":"Plain English with keywords and no variables, for a user to approve: structured English."},{"t":"IF nights > 7 THEN rate ← weeklyRate ELSE rate ← nightlyRate ENDIF","bin":2,"fb":"Variables and assignment arrows: pseudocode."},{"t":"REPEAT ask for the guest's surname UNTIL a matching booking is found, written for reception staff to confirm","bin":1,"fb":"A repetition structure in plain English, without variables: structured English."},{"t":"count ← 0 / REPEAT INPUT guestName / count ← count + 1 / UNTIL count = 10","bin":2,"fb":"A counter variable with assignment: pseudocode."},{"t":"A grid listing \"Member?\", \"Weekend?\" and \"Stay over 7 nights?\" with eight columns of Y/N entries","bin":3,"fb":"Conditions and 2 to the power 3 columns in a grid: a decision table."},{"t":"A diagram whose first branch asks \"Member?\" and whose second asks \"Weekend?\", with a rate at each of four end points","bin":4,"fb":"Branches and leaves: a decision tree."},{"t":"A definition stating that CheckInDate is stored as a date and must be today or later","bin":0,"fb":"A data item's type and validation rule: data dictionary."},{"t":"A diagram showing which rate applies, read from left to right, presented to the hotel's owners","bin":4,"fb":"A visual, ordered rule for non-technical readers: a decision tree."},{"t":"A grid in which two rules have been merged using a dash for \"Weekend?\"","bin":3,"fb":"Merging rules with a dash is done in a decision table."}],"win":"Well sorted. Data dictionary: definitions of data items. Structured English: plain-English logic for users. Pseudocode: logic with variables for programmers. Decision table: every combination in a grid. Decision tree: the same logic as ordered branches."}
