     "up": {"title":"Upper, lower or integrated CASE? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder features from Island Code Studio's CASE tools. Some upper-CASE checks sound technical, and some integrated features sound like simple automation. Ask which phases each feature serves.</p>","items":[{"t":"The tool checks the data dictionary and warns that \"RoomRate\" appears on a screen design but is never defined","bin":0,"fb":"Checking design documents for consistency is upper CASE."},{"t":"The tool compares two versions of the booking code line by line before they are merged","bin":1,"fb":"Comparing and merging code versions is version control: lower CASE."},{"t":"The project schedule, the diagrams, the code and the test results are held in one package and linked to each other","bin":2,"fb":"Linking every phase's work in one store is integrated CASE."},{"t":"The designer drags fields onto a clickable mock-up of the check-in screen for the owner to approve","bin":0,"fb":"Screen prototyping supports design: upper CASE."},{"t":"Automated tests run 120 checks on the Java fare routine every night","bin":1,"fb":"Automated testing is lower CASE."},{"t":"Deleting an entity from the ERD updates the database script and flags the test cases that used it","bin":2,"fb":"A design change flowing through to code and tests is the mark of integrated CASE."},{"t":"The analyst's tool flags a data store on a DFD that receives data but never sends any out","bin":0,"fb":"Checking a DFD for errors supports analysis: upper CASE."},{"t":"A documentation generator rebuilds the technical manual from the code comments after each change","bin":1,"fb":"Supporting maintenance with generated documentation is lower CASE."},{"t":"A UML class diagram of the booking objects is drawn and checked during design","bin":0,"fb":"Object modelling in design is upper CASE."},{"t":"A debugger lets a programmer pause the program and inspect the value of \"total\" line by line","bin":1,"fb":"Debugging belongs to development and testing: lower CASE."}],"win":"Well sorted. Upper CASE checks and draws the early work (diagrams, data dictionary, screen designs); lower CASE builds and checks the code (version control, tests, debuggers, generated manuals); integrated CASE links them so that a change in one phase flows through to the others."}
