     "up": {"title":"Which kind of CASE tool? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Island Code Studio's manager wants ONE CASE package for future projects. Her main requirement: when a field is renamed in the ER diagram, the data dictionary, the generated database tables and the technical manual must all update automatically, so that nothing drifts out of step.</p><p>Which type of CASE tool is she describing, and why?</p>","options":[{"t":"Upper CASE, because the change begins in an ER diagram","fb":"The change does start in an upper-CASE diagram, but carrying it through to code and manuals spans the later phases too."},{"t":"Integrated CASE, because it links the tools of every phase around one shared store, so a change in one place flows through to the others","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Traceability from diagram to code to manual, across the whole life cycle, is what integrated CASE provides."},{"t":"A UI prototyping tool, because users can see the change","fb":"Prototyping tools build clickable screens; they do not keep diagrams, code and manuals in step."},{"t":"Lower CASE, because it generates code and documentation","fb":"Code and documentation generators are lower-CASE features, but linking them to the ERD across the life cycle is integration."}]}
