     "up": {"title":"Prototyping the student portal (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are the analyst at Seaview Systems building a student portal for Sandbox Tree College. This time the decisions involve scope, accessibility and security, and some choices only half-work.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Students cannot say what they want on the timetable and grades pages, and registry staff have strong but different views. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Code the full portal from your own best guess.","to":"bad1","fb":"Without early feedback, requirement errors are found only after coding, when they are expensive to fix."},{"t":"Build a clickable prototype of both pages and test it with students AND registry staff, recording where their views differ.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. A prototype draws out requirements from both user groups, and recording differences prevents surprises later."},{"t":"Build a prototype and test it only with students, since they are the main users.","to":"ok1","fb":"A prototype is the right tool, but leaving out registry staff means their requirements surface late."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Users ask for 14 changes, including a mobile layout, a hurricane-closure banner, dark mode, a chat feature and live bus times. The budget allows one more round. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Build all 14 changes into the next round.","to":"ok2","fb":"Users are pleased, but the budget and timetable are now at risk."},{"t":"Agree priorities with the registrar: build the essential changes (mobile layout, closure banner) now and log the rest for a later release.","to":"n3","fb":"Right. Prioritising controls scope creep, a known risk of prototyping."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>A visually impaired student cannot use the grades page with her screen reader. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Note it for a future version.","to":"bad2","fb":"Leaving out a whole group of users is a usability failure, and it costs far more to fix after coding."},{"t":"Add accessibility (screen-reader labels and strong contrast) to the requirements and retest the prototype with her.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Usability for all students is a requirement, and the prototype is the cheapest place to fix it."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The prototype is a design-tool mock-up with no security or database behind it. The registrar wants to launch it next week. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Explain that it is a throwaway model; the real portal must be built with security and tested, and agree a realistic date.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You managed the classic limitation: users mistaking the prototype for the finished system."},{"t":"Launch the mock-up now and add security later.","to":"bad3","fb":"Quick prototypes skip security; putting one live exposes student data."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well prototyped.</b> You drew out requirements from both groups, controlled scope, built accessibility in early and refused to launch a model. The finished portal meets students' and staff needs and is secure.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Partly successful.</b> Students love the portal, but registry staff cannot record grade appeals, and that feature must be added after coding. Lesson: prototype with every user group.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Partly successful.</b> The portal has many features but launches two months late and over budget. Lesson: prototypes invite scope creep, so agree priorities.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Expensive rework.</b> Students reject the layout and days of coding must be redone. Try again and let users try a model first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded users.</b> After launch, the university must pay for a costly rebuild of the grades page so all students can use it. Try again and treat accessibility as a requirement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Insecure portal.</b> Student grades are exposed because the mock-up was never built with security. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}}
