     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> There are three yes/no conditions, so the number of rules = 2<sup>3</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 rules.</p><p><b>(b)</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Conditions</th><th>R1</th><th>R2</th><th>R3</th><th>R4</th><th>R5</th><th>R6</th><th>R7</th><th>R8</th></tr><tr><td>Student?</td><td>Y</td><td>Y</td><td>Y</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>N</td><td>N</td><td>N</td></tr><tr><td>Annual payment?</td><td>Y</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>N</td><td>Y</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>N</td></tr><tr><td>Joins a class?</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td><td>Y</td><td>N</td></tr><tr><th>Actions</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr><tr><td>Charge student rate</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Charge standard rate</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td>X</td></tr><tr><td>Give one free month</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Give free gym bag</td><td>X</td><td></td><td>X</td><td></td><td>X</td><td></td><td>X</td><td></td></tr></table></div><p>The condition stub and action stub are on the left; the Y/N condition entries use the halving pattern Y Y Y Y N N N N, Y Y N N Y Y N N, Y N Y N Y N Y N; an X marks each action that applies to a rule.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Development runs from week 5 to week 9, so its duration is 9 − 5 + 1 = 5 weeks. Overlapping tasks: Analysis and Design overlap in week 3; Development and Testing overlap in weeks 8 and 9. (Design and Development also overlap in week 5, and Testing and Implementation in week 10.)</p><p><b>(d)</b> A decision tree is a visual diagram in which each branch is a question and each path leads to the result. The gym's owner is not technical, so she can follow a path such as \"student, annual, joins a class\" from left to right and see the outcome, without having to learn how to read a grid of Y, N and X entries. The tree also shows the order in which the questions are asked at the front desk.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>In (a), one mark for 2<sup>3</sup> and one for 8. In (b), award one mark each for: correct conditions, correct actions, eight rules, a consistent Y/N pattern, correct rate X marks, correct free-month and gym-bag X marks. In (c), one mark for 5 weeks with working and up to three marks for correctly stated overlaps with weeks (any two of the four). In (d), credit visual/easy to follow plus a link to the non-technical owner.</p>",
