     "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Natural language, flowcharts and pseudocode.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> 1. Start.<br>2. Get the number of bicycles.<br>3. Get the number of hours.<br>4. Multiply the number of bicycles by the number of hours, then multiply by 12, to find the cost.<br>5. Display the cost.<br>6. Stop.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> START<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INPUT bikes<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INPUT hours<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;cost &larr; bikes * hours * 12<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT cost<br>STOP</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Terminal (oval): \"Start\" &rarr; input/output (parallelogram): \"Input bikes\" &rarr; input/output (parallelogram): \"Input hours\" &rarr; process (rectangle): \"cost = bikes * hours * 12\" &rarr; input/output (parallelogram): \"Output cost\" &rarr; terminal (oval): \"Stop\". The symbols are joined by flow lines (arrows) from top to bottom.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I recommend a <b>flowchart</b> for the owner. She has no programming experience, and a flowchart uses a small set of standard symbols joined by arrows, so she can follow the flow of control visually and check each step. <b>Whereas</b> pseudocode uses keywords such as INPUT and assignment statements such as cost &larr; bikes * hours * 12, which are designed for programmers and which she may find hard to read, so she might approve an error without noticing it.</p>",
     "teacherTip": "<p>For (b) and (c), credit any correct, general version, but deduct where students use fixed numbers (such as \"3 bikes for 2 hours\") instead of input data: that breaks the general-solution characteristic from SO 3.9. In (d), the shape names are what earn the marks. In (e), a recommendation of natural language is also acceptable if it is justified by audience and comparison.</p>",
