      "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The three ways are <b>natural language</b>, <b>flowcharts</b> and <b>pseudocode</b>.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> 1. Start. 2. Get the number of bicycles. 3. Get the number of hours. 4. Multiply the number of bicycles by the number of hours and by 12 to find the cost. 5. Display the cost. 6. Stop.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> START<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INPUT bikes<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INPUT hours<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;cost &larr; bikes * hours * 12<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;OUTPUT cost<br>STOP</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> (i) Start and Stop: <b>oval</b> (terminal). (ii) Input bikes: <b>parallelogram</b> (input/output). (iii) cost = bikes * hours * 12: <b>rectangle</b> (process).</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I would show the owner, who is not a programmer, a flowchart because she can follow the <b>symbols and arrows</b> visually and check each step without knowing programming keywords.</p>",
