     "up": {"title":"Which paradigm? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: these cards describe features and less familiar languages. Sort each one into its programming paradigm.</p>","items":[{"t":"A query asks for every booking after 1 June without saying how the data should be searched","bin":2,"fb":"Stating what is wanted, not how: declarative, as in SQL."},{"t":"Top-down design, with procedures called in turn from a main program","bin":0,"fb":"Step-by-step procedures: procedural."},{"t":"Encapsulation hides an object's data behind its methods","bin":1,"fb":"Encapsulation is an object-oriented feature."},{"t":"Functions are passed as values to other functions, and no variable is ever changed","bin":3,"fb":"Functions as values and no changing state: functional."},{"t":"Recursion is used instead of loops","bin":3,"fb":"Recursion in place of loops is typical of functional programming."},{"t":"COBOL","bin":0,"fb":"COBOL is a classic procedural language."},{"t":"C#","bin":1,"fb":"C# is object-oriented."},{"t":"Lisp","bin":3,"fb":"Lisp is a functional language."},{"t":"SQL","bin":2,"fb":"SQL is a declarative query language."},{"t":"A method called calculateFare() behaves differently for an AdultTicket object and a ChildTicket object","bin":1,"fb":"One method name behaving differently for different classes is polymorphism: object-oriented."}],"win":"Excellent. Declarative states what (SQL queries); procedural gives steps in procedures; object-oriented uses encapsulation and polymorphism; functional uses functions as values, recursion and no changing state."}
