      "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Pass boat 3: total = 40 + 75 = 115, 75 &gt; 50 is TRUE, big = 1. Pass boat 2: total = 145, 30 &gt; 50 is FALSE, big = 1. Pass boat 1: total = 200, 55 &gt; 50 is TRUE, big = 2.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The output is total / 4 = <b>50</b> and big = <b>2</b>.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The counter is <b>big</b> because it adds 1 each time a catch is over 50 kg. The accumulator is <b>total</b> because it adds each catch to a running total.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The algorithm is written before the program because it lets the programmer plan and test the logic with a trace table, so errors are found and fixed before any code is written.</p><p><b>(e)</b> C++ is an <b>object-oriented</b> language.</p>",
      "teacherTip": "<p>Give the first row of the table orally if a student is stuck, then ask them to say each new total aloud before writing it. Once they finish, move them on to part (b) of the full question: explaining the function of the algorithm in one sentence.</p>"
