  "stimulus": "<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p><p class=\"passage\"><b>Bayleaf Hill Secondary</b> in Grand Anse has one canteen and a 25-minute lunch break. Students queue for up to 15 minutes, and some leave without eating. The canteen manager, Mrs. Hodge, writes orders on paper and adds the bills by hand. The principal asks a team of CAPE IT students to solve the problem. They propose a pre-order system: students submit their order and student ID before 10:00 a.m.; the system checks the menu file, records the order, sends the kitchen an order list and gives the student a collection number. Students who pre-order three or more items receive a discount of 10 per cent.</p>",
  "prompt": "<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Explain what is meant by <b>problem-solving</b> as a process. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how the team would carry out <b>FOUR</b> stages of the problem-solving process for the canteen problem. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Draw a <b>context level DFD</b> (Gane and Sarson) for the pre-order system. Describe your diagram in words. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Develop a pseudocode algorithm that reads the number of items and the total cost of ONE student's order, applies the discount where the order has three or more items, and outputs the amount to pay. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>",
  "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Problem-solving is a process of ... in order to ...<br><b>(b)</b> Define the problem: ... Analyse: ... Identify and evaluate solutions: ... Select and justify: ... (Implement / Evaluate and review: ...)<br><b>(c)</b> [Diagram] Entities: Student, Kitchen ... Process 0: Canteen Pre-order System ... Flows: <i>order and student ID</i> from Student to process 0 ...<br><b>(d)</b> START / INPUT items, cost / IF items &gt;= 3 THEN ... ENDIF / OUTPUT ... / STOP</p>",
