  "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Problem-solving is a <b>systematic process</b>: a series of logical stages that move from recognising that a problem exists to finding, implementing and reviewing a <b>solution</b>, rather than guessing at a fix.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Define the problem:</b> write a clear problem statement, for example \"Students wait up to 15 minutes for lunch, so some leave without eating.\" <b>Analyse the problem:</b> gather information by timing the queue, interviewing Mrs. Hodge and surveying students to find the causes, such as manual adding of bills. <b>Identify and evaluate possible solutions:</b> list options such as a second serving point, pre-packed meals or a pre-order system, and evaluate each for cost and time saved. <b>Select and justify the optimal solution:</b> choose the pre-order system because it removes ordering and calculation from the lunch break at low cost. (Then implement it and evaluate and review whether queues are shorter.)</p><p><b>(c)</b> [Diagram described] One rounded-rectangle process labelled <b>0 Canteen Pre-order System</b> in the centre. External entity <b>Student</b> (square) on the left: data flow <i>order details and student ID</i> from Student to process 0; data flow <i>collection number</i> from process 0 to Student. External entity <b>Kitchen</b> (square) on the right: data flow <i>order list</i> from process 0 to Kitchen. External entity <b>Canteen Manager</b>: data flow <i>daily menu</i> to process 0 and <i>sales summary</i> back. No data stores are shown inside a context diagram; the menu and order files appear at Level 1.</p><p><b>(d)</b></p><pre>START\n  INPUT items, cost\n  IF items &gt;= 3 THEN\n     pay = cost * 0.9\n  ELSE\n     pay = cost\n  ENDIF\n  OUTPUT \"Amount to pay: \", pay\nSTOP</pre>",
  "teacherTip": "<p>Common errors: describing the SDLC (feasibility, design) instead of the problem-solving stages; drawing several processes or data stores on a context diagram; unlabelled arrows; labelling a process with a noun (\"Orders\") instead of a verb phrase; algorithms with no input statement, or with the discount applied to every order because the IF is missing.</p>",
